==English==

===Etymology===
{{PIE root|en|h₁es}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|potens|potens, potentem|powerful, strong, potent}}, present participle of {{m|la|posse||to be able}}, from {{m|la|potis||able, powerful, originally a lord, master}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˈpəʊt(ə)nt/|lang=en}}
* {{a|GA}} {{enPR|pōtʹnt}} {{IPA|/ˈpoʊt(ə)nt/|[ˈpʰoʊ̯ʔn̩t]|[-n̩ʔ]|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|əʊtənt|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|po|tent|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Possessing [[strength]].
#: {{ux|en|a '''potent''' argument}}
#* {{RQ:Vance Nobody|I}}
#*: Little disappointed, then, she turned attention to "Chat of the Social World," gossip which exercised '''potent''' fascination upon the girl's intelligence.
# Being [[effective]], causing body effects.
#: {{ux|en|a '''potent''' medicine}}
# Having a [[sharp]] or [[offensive]] taste.
# {{lb|en|of a male}} Able to [[procreate]].
# [[very|Very]] [[powerful]] or [[effective]].
#* {{w|William Shakespeare}} (1564-1616)
#*: harsh and '''potent''' injuries
#* {{w|John Milton}} (1608-1674)
#*: Moses once more his '''potent''' rod extends.

